---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import ImagePlaceholder from "../../components/ImagePlaceholder.astro";
import Icon from "../../components/Icon.astro";
import MailButton from "../../components/MailButton.astro";
import poohPlanning from "../../assets/images/illustrations/pooh-planning.webp";
import { enFactors, enBudgetFaq } from "../../data/en/budget";
import { enBreadcrumb, enFaqPage } from "../../utils/schema-en";
import { EMAIL } from "../../../site.config.mjs";

const faqSchema = enFaqPage(enBudgetFaq);
const breadcrumb = enBreadcrumb(
	Astro.site,
	"What drives the budget",
	"/en/budget-factors/"
);
---

<Layout
	lang="en"
	title="What Affects the Cost of a Website or System | Pooh"
	description="Six things affect what a website or back-office system costs, and scope matters most. A plain explanation from a freelance developer in Bangkok, Thailand, so you can compare quotes fairly. Email for an estimate."
	schema={[faqSchema, breadcrumb]}
>
	<section class="page-head band-cream">
		<div class="shell page-head-inner">
			<p class="label">Budget</p>
			<h1>What affects the cost, and why quotes can look so different</h1>
			<p class="lede">
				<strong
					>I cannot give you a number before I have seen your project, and I
					would be guessing if I tried.</strong
				> "A website" covers everything from a single page to a system a whole company
				runs on, so two quotes for what sounds like the same request can be
				very far apart. Here is what actually moves it, so you have something
				concrete to compare against.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<div class="shell">
			<div class="budget-overview">
				<div class="section-head reveal">
					<p class="label">Six things that move it</p>
					<h2>Why two projects that sound identical are not</h2>
				</div>

				<div class="budget-visual reveal reveal-d1">
					<ImagePlaceholder
						src={poohPlanning}
						alt="Three dimensional illustration of the Pooh mascot planning out a project scope"
						label="Pooh mascot planning a project scope"
						ratio="16 / 10"
						loading="eager"
						fetchpriority="high"
						sizes="(min-width: 68rem) 32rem, (min-width: 48rem) 44vw, calc(100vw - 2.75rem)"
					/>
				</div>
			</div>

			<ol class="factors">
				{
					enFactors.map((item, i) => (
						<li class:list={["factor", "reveal", `reveal-d${i % 3}`]}>
							<span class="factor-n" aria-hidden="true">
								{String(i + 1).padStart(2, "0")}
							</span>
							<div class="factor-body">
								<h3>{item.title}</h3>
								<p>{item.body}</p>
							</div>
						</li>
					))
				}
			</ol>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section band-tint">
		<div class="shell narrow">
			{/* Practical advice a customer can act on, including advice that
			    costs him work. An earlier draft made the second item an argument
			    for hiring in Bangkok, which is the same point the home page
			    makes; per ADR 0002 that point is made once and not repeated on
			    every page. */}
			<div class="section-head reveal">
				<p class="label">Keeping the cost down</p>
				<h2>Two things that genuinely help</h2>
			</div>

			<div class="lever-list reveal">
				<div class="lever">
					<h3>Start with the smallest version that is actually useful</h3>
					<p>
						The most expensive way to build software is to plan all of it,
						build all of it, and find out at the end that a third of it was
						wrong. It is usually better to build the part that has to work,
						put it in front of real people, and let what they do decide what
						comes next. You spend less, and you are up and running much
						sooner.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="lever">
					<h3>Tell me what you are not sure about</h3>
					<p>
						If part of the plan is a guess, say so. I would much rather leave
						it out of this round and add it once you know, than build
						something we both suspect will change. Quite often the most useful
						thing I can tell you is that a piece does not need building yet at
						all, and I would rather say that than quietly put it on the list.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<div class="shell narrow">
			<div class="section-head reveal">
				<p class="label">Common questions</p>
				<h2>What people ask about money</h2>
			</div>

			<div class="faq">
				{
					enBudgetFaq.map((item, i) => (
						<details class:list={["faq-item", "reveal", `reveal-d${i % 3}`]}>
							<summary>
								<span class="faq-q">{item.q}</span>
								<span class="faq-sign" aria-hidden="true" />
							</summary>
							<p class="faq-a">{item.a}</p>
						</details>
					))
				}
			</div>

			<p class="more reveal">
				Want to see what a system like this involves? <a href="/en/backoffice/"
					>Here is what I build</a
				>, and <a href="/en/work/">here is what it has looked like</a>.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="band">
		<div class="shell band-inner reveal">
			<p class="label">Get an estimate</p>
			<h2>Tell me about it and I will give you a rough idea</h2>
			<p class="lede">
				You should not have to guess at this on your own. Tell me what your
				business does and what you would like built, and I will come back with
				the size of the job, what I would leave out to bring it down, and
				anything I think you do not need yet.
			</p>
			<div class="actions">
				<MailButton label={`Email ${EMAIL}`} tone="on-dark" />
				<a class="cta-quiet" href="/en/contact/">
					Other ways to reach me
					<Icon name="arrow" size={18} />
				</a>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</Layout>

<style>
	.narrow {
		max-width: 48rem;
	}

	.page-head .lede strong {
		color: var(--ink);
		font-weight: 500;
	}

	.budget-overview {
		display: grid;
		gap: 2rem;
		margin-bottom: 2.25rem;
	}

	.budget-overview .section-head {
		margin-bottom: 0;
	}

	.budget-visual {
		min-width: 0;
	}

	.budget-visual :global(.ph) {
		overflow: hidden;
		border-radius: var(--radius);
	}

	@media (min-width: 48em) {
		.budget-overview {
			grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.9fr) minmax(0, 1.1fr);
			gap: 3.5rem;
			align-items: center;
			margin-bottom: 3.5rem;
		}
	}

	/* ---- factors ----
	   The page argues that no single number exists because six things move it,
	   so the six read as one continuous scale rather than six equal cards: a
	   single column with an unbroken rule running its full height and a honey
	   tick at each stop. The ticks stay the same size on purpose, because the
	   copy names scope as the largest driver and it comes first; a mark that
	   grew downward would state the opposite of what the page says. */

	.factors {
		display: grid;
		gap: 0;
		margin: 0;
		padding: 0;
		list-style: none;
		max-width: 44rem;
	}

	.factor {
		position: relative;
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: auto minmax(0, 1fr);
		gap: 0 1.25rem;
		align-items: start;
		min-width: 0;
		padding: 1.25rem 0 1.5rem;
	}

	.factor::before {
		content: "";
		position: absolute;
		left: 0.53125rem;
		top: 0;
		bottom: 0;
		width: 1px;
		background: var(--rule);
	}

	.factor:first-child::before {
		top: 1.5rem;
	}

	.factor:last-child::before {
		bottom: auto;
		height: 1.5rem;
	}

	.factor-n {
		position: relative;
		z-index: 1;
		display: grid;
		justify-items: center;
		gap: 0.375rem;
		width: 1.0625rem;
		padding-block: 0.9375rem;
		font-family: var(--font-head);
		font-weight: 600;
		font-size: var(--fs-label);
		line-height: 1;
		color: var(--honey-ink);
		font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
		background: var(--cream);
	}

	.factor-n::before {
		content: "";
		width: 0.6875rem;
		height: 3px;
		border-radius: 2px;
		background: var(--honey);
	}

	.factor-body {
		display: grid;
		gap: 0.375rem;
		min-width: 0;
	}

	.factor-body h3 {
		font-size: var(--fs-subtitle);
		font-weight: 600;
	}

	.factor-body p {
		color: var(--muted);
		font-size: var(--fs-body-sm);
		max-width: 38rem;
	}

	@media (min-width: 48em) {
		.factor {
			gap: 0 1.75rem;
			padding: 1.5rem 0 1.75rem;
		}
	}

	/* ---- the two levers ----
	   Deliberately two blocks, not a card grid: these are arguments to read,
	   not options to pick between. */
	.lever-list {
		display: grid;
		gap: 1.75rem;
	}

	.lever {
		display: grid;
		gap: 0.5rem;
	}

	.lever h3 {
		font-size: var(--fs-subtitle);
		font-weight: 600;
	}

	.lever p {
		color: var(--muted);
		max-width: 38rem;
	}

	.faq-a {
		max-width: 40rem;
	}
</style>
